/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The gear lever sends an electrical command to the gear control unit (on the A320 family, two landing gear control and interface units working in turn).
   - A squat switch (weight-on-wheels sensor) on the shock strut senses that the strut is compressed.
   - The gear warning sounds when the gear is not down and locked and the throttles are near idle at low height, the usual sign of an approach.
   - If hydraulic pressure is lost, alternate extension releases the uplocks, by a cable, an electric motor or a gas bottle, and the gear falls under its own weight.
   - Trailing-edge Fowler flaps move aft and down, adding area and camber.
   - Both wings must move together.
   - Load limiting protects the structure.
   - Angle of attack α is the angle between the wing chord line and the relative airflow.
   - VS is the stall speed in m/s; W is the weight in N; ρ is the air density in kg/m³; S is the wing area in m²; CLmax is the maximum lift coefficient, which rises when flaps are extended.
   - On a light aircraft the warning comes from a small flapper switch on the wing leading edge: near the stall the stagnation point moves down and back, the airflow lifts the flapper and the switch sounds a horn.
*/
