/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Most airframe icing happens between 0 °C and about −20 °C in cloud or rain holding supercooled droplets: water still liquid below freezing.
   - Vstall is the stall speed in m/s; W is the aircraft weight in N, including the ice; ρ is air density in kg/m³; S is wing area in m²; CLmax is the maximum lift coefficient, which falls when the leading edge is rough.
   - Ice also costs time: after freezing rain the aircraft needs fluid sprayed by a de-icing rig before departure, and the crew checks the fluid's holdover time.
   - Two words carry most of the marks.
   - Bleed air is not free.
   - Pneumatic boots: an older rule told crews to wait for 6 to 13 mm of ice before the first cycle, for fear of ice bridging (a thin layer stretching with the boot and staying on it).
   - Protection is fitted where ice does the most harm, not over the whole airframe; weight and cost forbid that.
   - Detection can be visual: a wing inspection light lets the crew see ice at night.
   - Rain removal works on the windscreen.
*/
