/* book exam traps: Calling a pneumatic boot anti-icing. It is de-icing: it lets ice form and then cracks it off. | Listing only the wing when asked where protection is fitted. Tailplane, engine inlet, propeller, pitot and static heads, windscreen and drain masts all count. | Writing that boots must always wait for a thick layer. That is old guidance; modern boots are cycled from the first sign of ice unless the flight manual says otherwise. | Blaming the ice weight for the higher stall speed. A few kilograms of ice matter far less than the lost CLmax; and 3 mm is 0.003 m, not 0.03 m, in the mass sum. | Forgetting that bleed-air anti-icing steals compressor air, so thrust falls and fuel burn rises while the system is on. */
